WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.040
- We have lived for a hundred
years on the assumption of

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.780
if it's on film it's true,

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.890
and we are about to enter the
era of that not being true.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:09.885
(soft upbeat music)

00:00:09.885 --> 00:00:10.830
(cheering)

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.293
You've got your perspective.

00:00:13.317 --> 00:00:16.045
(crowd cheering)

00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:16.878
I just want to be happy.

00:00:16.878 --> 00:00:17.850
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:22.320
Hey guys.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.990
Welcome to a very special
episode of the podcast in Cannes.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:28.990
I'm here with Kevin Allocca.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.920
I'm gonna allow him to kind
of give you the context.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.700
I have a series of questions,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.070
but this kicks off our
interview series at Cannes.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.560
Something that I wanted to
do for a lot of this audience

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.570
as the podcast has grown
and become far more 360.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.150
Obviously so much grounded
in entrepreneurship

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.370
and startup culture but
over the last half decade

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:53.565
a lot more in the ad world
and the Fortune 500 land.

00:00:53.565 --> 00:00:54.970
And what's really excited
about having Kevin on

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.440
is I think his organization,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.400
and really ironically what he
actually does for a living,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.680
I think, touches a far majority
of the people listening.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.630
So I'm super excited, bro.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.140
I'm really glad you're here.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.210
Please tell the Vaynernation
who you are and what you do.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.043
- Sure.

00:01:10.043 --> 00:01:11.323
So I'm Kevin Allocca.

00:01:11.323 --> 00:01:14.090
I'm the Head of Culture
and Trends at YouTube.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:16.540
I basically work on projects
that help people find

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.140
and understand what's popular on YouTube

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.900
at the end of the day,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.050
and I've been tracking trends at YouTube

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.540
for almost nine years now.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.290
I wrote a book called "Videocracy"

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.310
which looks at like ways
that people interact

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.790
with culture and interact with video

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.260
and how that influences culture.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.940
And so basically I work on,

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:37.300
like on the one side we do a
lot of like consumer stuff,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.960
basically how we surface and understand

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.660
what's popular on YouTube,

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:42.493
and then on the other side,

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:44.950
like I am here because a lot of times

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.590
people won't understand in this world

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.313
what's going on in creativity,
what's going on on YouTube--

00:01:49.313 --> 00:01:50.800
- I mean, this world,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.350
this world needs to
pay way more attention.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.450
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:54.283
- You agree with that?

00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:55.116
- I do, I mean.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.380
We were at this phase a few years ago

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.130
where it was like, okay,
there's a lot of audience here,

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:02.970
so, we're gonna pay attention

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.020
to that audience but not
take a second to understand

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.120
what they're watching or
why they're watching it.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.180
And now we're kind of
entering this other phase

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.984
where I think everybody's starting to see,

00:02:10.984 --> 00:02:13.610
like to understand how
to reach that audience,

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:14.970
I need to understand why they're there,

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.868
what they're doing there,
who they care about.

00:02:17.868 --> 00:02:19.390
You know, I'm gonna
give this talk this week

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:20.590
about this in many ways.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:24.520
But we're so used to making
assumptions and value judgments

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.837
about about creativity and about content

00:02:26.837 --> 00:02:29.630
and and what people should
like or shouldn't like.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:30.680
And YouTube subverts.
- It's audacious.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.730
- A lot of that stuff.
- It's audacious.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:35.640
You know, so let's take a step back.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.540
What kind of kid were you?

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:39.190
Then where'd you grow up?

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:40.520
Like where you into Star Wars

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.690
or like selling Jolly Ranchers?

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.320
I see you have two ear ring holes?

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:46.930
- No, no, no, it's just a beauty mark.

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.270
I never.
- Oh, those are just?

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.903
- Yeah, yeah.
- Those are not?

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:51.900
Those are just birthmarks?
- Yeah, that's right.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:52.740
- All right, damn it.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.750
I was like why'd you have
two earrings in the same ear?

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:55.914
I was super fired up.

00:02:55.914 --> 00:02:57.530
That was like I went to it immediately.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.890
- But I did grow up in like
north of Miami in South Florida.

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:02.520
- Okay, what town?
- Which is interesting place.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.513
Hollywood, Florida.
- Yeah.

00:03:03.513 --> 00:03:04.760
That's where all the Russians have gone!

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.170
Do you know about this?

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.430
Like all my parent's generation Russians

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.390
have all moved to this Hollywood, Florida.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.290
It's about to be the
Brighton Beach of Florida.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.390
- I was part of the wave where
everybody's Italian parents

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:16.640
moved to Florida and stuff.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.240
- Makes sense.
- That's how I was born there.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.560
And I grew up there until
I was 18 and I was like,

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.356
I gotta get out of here.
- What kind of kid were you?

00:03:23.356 --> 00:03:26.150
- I kept trying to figure out what.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.660
In between cultures, in a way.
- How old are you right now?

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:28.840
- I'm 35.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:29.673
- Keep going.

00:03:29.673 --> 00:03:31.250
- So, I was in between cultures in a way

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.490
because everybody I grew up with was like

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.610
kids of first generation immigrants

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.810
from Latin America and things.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.220
But my family was from New York

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.210
and they were Italian American.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.310
So, I just was like, I'm
going to be into metal

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:44.890
'cause that's like the last.
- That's what you decided?

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.347
- Thing that you should be
into when you're in Miami,

00:03:46.347 --> 00:03:48.040
you know, south Florida.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.760
'Cause I just wanted to be--
- You wanna be countercultural

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:50.870
- Yeah, exactly, you know.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.010
- So what did you get into?
- And I was interested--

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.652
- Iron Maiden and shit?
- In theater and stuff.

00:03:53.652 --> 00:03:56.000
Metallica, Pantera, and I
listen to that stuff now,

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.546
I'm like, oh God.

00:03:57.546 --> 00:03:58.379
You know?

00:03:58.379 --> 00:03:59.212
Metallica, okay.

00:03:59.212 --> 00:04:00.045
But some of the other stuff.

00:04:00.045 --> 00:04:00.878
- Rat.
- Yeah.

00:04:04.425 --> 00:04:07.040
It took me a long time to embrace that

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.280
I just really actually loved
what I was around, which was

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:10.750
all the hip-hop scene that
was happening down there.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:11.960
- Interesting.
- I know.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.081
By the time I went to college--

00:04:13.081 --> 00:04:13.914
- Literally, you were that kind of dude,

00:04:13.914 --> 00:04:15.330
like you wanted to be counter cultural.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.860
So you didn't wanna be into
hip hop like everybody else.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:19.810
- Yeah.
- I love that.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:21.260
Oh I know people like that.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.470
- Everybody's into Wu Tang.
- Steve Unwin.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.190
- I was like, I don't
wanna get into Wu Tang.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.194
But now I'm like, "man,
Wu Tang was so good."

00:04:26.194 --> 00:04:27.440
- So good.
- It took me so long

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.052
to sort of really appreciate
some of these things.

00:04:30.052 --> 00:04:30.960
- So that's when you were into music?

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.140
- Yeah.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.040
- What were your other interests?

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.790
- I really wanted to work in the movies

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.070
for a long long time.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.580
I did theater and speech
and debate and stuff.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.730
Nerd stuff.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:42.190
- But you were outward?

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.303
- Yeah, totally.

00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:46.890
- You know, speech and debate,
historically nerd stuff

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:48.760
but it's so crazy now that
everybody wants to be.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.280
Like all these 18 year olds think I'm cool

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.080
'cause I give motivational speeches.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:54.337
And they're all like hitting
me up and they're like,

00:04:54.337 --> 00:04:55.617
"I wanna join the debate team."

00:04:55.617 --> 00:04:57.040
"I wanna join the speech."

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.370
And I'm like, my God.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.630
This was the nerdiest
shit when I was a kid

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.105
and now just how the world moves.

00:05:03.105 --> 00:05:04.784
- I mean, and one of the
most invaluable things--

00:05:04.784 --> 00:05:05.720
- If you think about you right now,

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.280
you are like textbook influencer.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.510
Every kid at 14 wants to be an influencer

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:10.630
on Earth.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:13.120
And literally being into
film, speech, and debate

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.884
would be like a real good idea.

00:05:14.884 --> 00:05:16.140
- It's so correct.
- You like set the tone

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.880
for the framework.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.120
This is actually kind of meta.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:20.730
This is why I love asking
questions like this.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:22.160
You're sitting in this
incredibly important

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.400
position at YouTube,

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.110
which is really outside of Instagram,

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.630
the two places where the
manifestation of influencer,

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.610
the way every 14 year old thinks.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:32.870
And literally the framework
of what you grew up

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:35.500
actually is probably the blueprint

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.010
a lot of them should be thinking about.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:37.843
- It's true.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:41.240
And I did comedy for a bit, too.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.700
And those years that I
spent doing that stuff

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.050
like really shaped.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:46.180
- Comedy for sure

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:47.013
Improv?

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:48.840
- Sketch.
- Yeah, same shit though

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:49.673
at some level.

00:05:49.673 --> 00:05:50.506
- Yeah, totally.

00:05:50.506 --> 00:05:51.410
But it's all about trying to

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:52.950
understand an audience in a way.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.520
- That's exactly right.
- It took me a long time

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.560
to figure out that.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.470
But I was really just obsessed
with why people like stuff

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:58.857
and that is professionally
my job now is like

00:05:58.857 --> 00:06:00.090
how do people like stuff?
- Good for you.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.450
How do you end up in this position?

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:03.283
- It's pretty weird.

00:06:03.283 --> 00:06:04.400
- You've been at YouTube for nine years?

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.233
- Yes.

00:06:05.233 --> 00:06:06.820
So, before that I was a
comedy writer for a bit

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:07.970
at the Huffington Post.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:09.703
And then I worked at Mediabistro.

00:06:09.703 --> 00:06:11.890
You remember Mediabistro?
- Yeah, very well, very well.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:13.026
Yeah, of course.

00:06:13.026 --> 00:06:15.070
- So, I was their television
news industry editor for a bit.

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:16.926
Like after Stelter (murmurs) TVNewser

00:06:16.926 --> 00:06:19.447
- You worked for Jonah at HuffPo?

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:21.743
- There was this thing

00:06:21.743 --> 00:06:23.590
with Barry Diller.
- Paul Berry?

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:24.450
- Paul was there when I was there.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.070
But Barry Diller and Ariana did this thing

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.330
called 23/6 which is
like a political satire

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:29.400
thing for two years.
- Yes I remember, I remember.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.280
- And that was my first job in New York.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.190
I was an editorial assistant.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.840
I worked my way up to
associate editor or whatever

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.520
during the '08 election run
up and right afterwards.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:37.560
Like I didn't really appreciate

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:38.600
the economics of it at the time.

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:41.380
So I was baffled when the thing
fell apart two years later.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:42.680
Now I'm like, "of course".

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.500
- That's about to happen in
the world again pretty soon.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:47.870
Everyone's about to be baffled.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:48.947
- Yeah.

00:06:48.947 --> 00:06:50.927
- "How's my directed
consumer brand not doing well

00:06:50.927 --> 00:06:53.840
"even though I'm losing money
on every customer I acquire?"

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:55.497
- I can't imagine how we made it work

00:06:55.497 --> 00:06:57.577
for as long as they did.

00:06:57.577 --> 00:06:58.898
- Well, Barry has deep pockets.

00:06:58.898 --> 00:06:59.843
- I guess that helps.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:05.570
I knew a lot about pop culture
from working in that job.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:06.460
- By the way, real quick on that,

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.550
just as now selfish for my audience,

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.900
back to Barry and his deep pockets.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:10.930
It's actually not that.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.491
It's actually a lot of what I think about.

00:07:12.491 --> 00:07:14.910
I have so many things micro fail

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:17.100
but the macro always works.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.569
You know, if you think about (murmurs)

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:19.402
you think about all that,

00:07:19.402 --> 00:07:21.850
they're doing so much that all they need

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:23.223
is three or four wins.

00:07:24.591 --> 00:07:28.740
23/6 is right, it's just timing
and the economy and all that

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.880
didn't allow them to see it through.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.020
But it's really fascinating

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:35.373
when you look at an icon
like Barry or others,

00:07:36.234 --> 00:07:37.950
they're always trying things.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.580
And nobody here knows about 23/6

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:42.230
but everybody knows about

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.290
the biggest dating
ecosystem on the internet.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:47.320
Everybody knows about all the other things

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:48.153
that they've accomplished.

00:07:48.153 --> 00:07:50.510
College Humor and all those iconic things.

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:51.343
You know?

00:07:51.343 --> 00:07:52.176
- And there were a lot of things

00:07:52.176 --> 00:07:53.130
that we were doing back then.

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.506
Like these kind of
political supercut things

00:07:55.506 --> 00:07:58.456
in that kind of remix style
that YouTube had just launched.

00:07:59.929 --> 00:08:01.360
- Were you doing Deepfakes yet?

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:02.360
- No.
- Okay.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.550
- We were several years
away from that treatment.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.300
- D-Rock, when are you
gonna make Deepfakes of me

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.110
saying shit like, "I love Tom Brady"?

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:10.240
By the way, for everybody who knows,

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:11.610
that will never happen.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:14.150
So that will be a Deepfake.

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:15.240
- Just warning everybody now.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.700
- By the way, just like
to be completely scattered

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:18.780
like I like to be.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.950
This Deepfake thing really is fascinating.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:23.240
- It is.
- Like if you think about

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.120
AI's advancement, I'm like wow.

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:27.620
We're talking about a half decade from now

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:29.460
where this shit's gonna be perfect

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:30.333
and now what?

00:08:33.410 --> 00:08:34.243
It's so funny,

00:08:34.243 --> 00:08:37.053
the openness of the internet
was something I understood.

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:39.920
Like I almost feel like I'm just gonna be

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.540
live-streaming at all
times to prove it's me

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:43.856
and what I have to say.

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:45.590
Everybody thinks it's gonna close.

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:47.280
I even just started saying the close-ness.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.360
My counter move to the
close-ness is complete on-ness.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.193
- Yeah.

00:08:53.246 --> 00:08:55.910
- If I stream every minute of my life,

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:57.637
there should be no
debate if I ever said it

00:08:57.637 --> 00:09:00.090
'cause it's documented if I did or didn't.

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:00.923
I can prove that I didn't.

